{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ef7880e1-d2a0-4673-9da7-65acdc83a065","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A point travels counterclockwise around a unit circle. We will graph its vertical coordinate against the angle turned.","ops":[{"op":"diagram","id":"circle","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"ellipse","center":[0.5,0.43],"radius":[0.32,0.32],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"Unit circle centered at the origin"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.43],[0.9,0.43]],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.06],[0.5,0.8]],"stroke":"solid"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.87,0.48],"content":"x"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.55,0.07],"content":"y"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.45,0.48],"content":"0"}]},{"op":"axes","id":"waveaxes","region":"right","x":{"min":0,"max":6.283185307,"label":"θ (radians)"},"y":{"min":-1.3,"max":1.3,"label":"y"}}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ef7880e1-d2a0-4673-9da7-65acdc83a065","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The radius is one. At angle theta, the point's height is sine theta; here, thirty degrees gives a height of one-half.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"radius","canvas_id":"circle","from":[0.5,0.43],"to":[0.7771,0.27],"stroke":"solid","label":"1"},{"op":"line","id":"height","canvas_id":"circle","from":[0.7771,0.43],"to":[0.7771,0.27],"stroke":"dashed","label":"y"},{"op":"angle_arc","id":"angle","canvas_id":"circle","center":[0.5,0.43],"radius":0.12,"start_deg":-30,"end_deg":0,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ"},{"op":"equation","id":"height_eq","anchor":{"el":"circle","side":"below","gap":0.03},"latex":"y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac12"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"ef7880e1-d2a0-4673-9da7-65acdc83a065","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s3"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ef7880e1-d2a0-4673-9da7-65acdc83a065","step":{"id":"s3","script":"As the point completes one turn, its height rises, falls through zero, becomes negative, and returns to zero. Those heights trace the sine wave.","ops":[{"op":"curve","id":"sine","axes_id":"waveaxes","expr":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.283185307]},{"op":"equation","id":"landmarks","anchor":{"el":"waveaxes","side":"below","gap":0.03},"latex":"\\sin(0)=0,\\quad\\sin(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\\sin(\\pi)=0,\\quad\\sin(3\\pi/2)=-1,\\quad\\sin(2\\pi)=0"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ef7880e1-d2a0-4673-9da7-65acdc83a065","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The circle's radius sets the amplitude, and one full turn sets the period. Which angle gives the lowest point of this wave?","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"summary","anchor":{"el":"landmarks","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"\\text{Amplitude}=1,\\qquad \\text{Period}=2\\pi"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Between zero and two pi, at which angle does sine reach its minimum, and what is that minimum?","expected_gist":"At 3π/2, the point is at the bottom of the circle and sine equals −1."}}}
